    "final_answer": "In most replication systems, the replica promoted during a failover is typically determined by:\n\n1. **Highest log position / lowest lag** - The replica that has processed the most transactions (highest WAL position in PostgreSQL, highest binlog position in MySQL) is usually chosen as it has the least data to catch up.\n\n2. **Priority settings** - Some systems allow you to configure replica priorities where higher-priority replicas are preferred for promotion.\n\n3. **Last known healthy state** - The system may check which replica was last receiving writes successfully and promote that one.\n\n4. **Automatic failover configurations** - Systems like PostgreSQL streaming replication, MySQL MHA, or managed services (AWS RDS, Google Cloud SQL) have built-in logic to select the best candidate based on their health checks and replication lag metrics.\n\nCould you clarify which specific system or database you're asking about? The exact promotion criteria can vary between:\n- PostgreSQL streaming replication\n- MySQL/MariaDB replication\n- Redis Sentinel/Cluster\n- MongoDB replica sets\n- Or other distributed systems"
